{"step_index":27,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:12:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-10T06:42:51+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-10T06:42:57+05:30\nThe search for \"Multi-Head Latent Attention MLA DeepSeek KV cache compression technique VRAM savings 2025 2026\" returned the following summary:\n**Multi-Head Latent Attention (MLA)** is a memory-efficient attention mechanism introduced by DeepSeek (first appearing in DeepSeek-V2 and utilized in V3 and R1) to solve the \"KV cache bottleneck\" in Large Language Models (LLMs)[1][2][3].\n\n### How MLA Works: KV Cache Compression\nIn standard Multi-Head Attention (MHA), the Key (K) and Value (V) states for every token are stored in VRAM, which grows linearly with sequence length and quickly exhausts GPU memory[1][4]. \n\nMLA reduces this memory footprint through two primary techniques:\n\n1.  **Low-Rank Key-Value Compression:** Instead of storing large, high-dimensional K and V vectors for every head, MLA projects these into a single, compact **shared latent vector**[1][5][6]. Only this significantly smaller latent vector is stored in the KV cache[5][7].\n2.  **On-Demand Reconstruction (Weight Absorption):** During inference, the model reconstructs the full K and V vectors from the compressed latent state as needed[2][6]. A \"weight absorption\" trick allows these projection matrices to be folded into other parts of the attention mechanism, minimizing the computational overhead of this reconstruction[5][8][9].\n\n### VRAM Savings and Performance (2025–2026)\n*   **Massive Footprint Reduction:** MLA has been reported to reduce the KV cache size by **up to ~93% to 98%** compared to standard Multi-Head Attention (MHA)[9][10][6].\n*   **Increased Concurrency:** By drastically shrinking the cache, MLA allows for much longer context lengths and higher user concurrency on the same hardware[11][6]. For example, a system that could only handle 1–2 users at a specific context length with GQA (Grouped-Query Attention) may be able to support 8–9 users using MLA with the same memory budget[6].\n*   **Performance vs. GQA:** While GQA is a popular alternative that shares KV heads to save memory, it can sometimes lead to slight degradation in model quality[2][12]. MLA maintains higher modeling performance than MHA while simultaneously achieving superior memory efficiency compared to GQA, making it a highly desirable architecture for modern, long-context LLMs[2][13][14].\n\n### Wider Adoption\nBy 2025 and 2026, the success of DeepSeek's models has led to increased interest in migrating other models to this structure[13][10]. Tools and frameworks like **TransMLA** have been developed to convert GQA-based models (like LLaMA or Mistral) into an MLA-compatible format to leverage these VRAM savings and inference speedups[13][10]. Additionally, optimized backends like **vLLM** and **SGLang** have integrated support for MLA to maximize throughput on modern GPUs (such as H200s and Blackwell architectures)[13][11][6].\n\nSources:\n[1] [medium.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEQooOb30RuIUsvkcc8Urt3m6OJ6T49Vu7UgHofKMiXmPPdIIfdYQomeAZ7u_BOnASUjjMNe1ISqiShCvBWNOq5P6gWYz-00MNuQHuNJyMo2wssk3jh60Kgjef6rsz2h7LYK23qw5bh5nXDA1gDkcIo09-XI2MmxaqNeeKSaAABRJVCNjjEQfPgvNhNeonObkBqslWEJxNAs7TUPmCC0gxyeX5d-Ob066RIjEMh2gEn7z0WQWlT)\n[2] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH8MQXRp_VwFghYtlUbNkPTrMOltJUenBttaAhaPNiXbnM7nlfZZnYAs-sOwlcoNzuAUZfQZx_WYXltWHgmeBuUGRVeKS63thfOy5vliOdVUMwZcNsNrrr7tGjOcbkEhr94yYeF0WscPAms0dTq_JVPSX39mq8Ln36UbPEYy0Q8Lw==)\n[3] [github.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEbKKvQ7ZF8DZiv8Djm12OhkpW5uNg8MoOv_cS4vINB56La3wc3QGqG8TYuIcYTsZer4vphF90an6k3aikk8yCN-KIGYqq-6J7PMdHzxgTbuqrIlSW-CShlhKQ8_k_DmkkzaoJoN9wLzAr2tE_kuLn-bCX-1TIZ)\n[4] [bonginn.me](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFKq2bX7nhDUYlwXoFWCLVesL3T